            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+space"} symmetric space
          %p
            Initially introduced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Tits"} Jacques Tits
            as a means to understand the structure of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+group"} exceptional Lie group
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+space"} homogeneous space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
          %blockquote
            %p
              (for the moment the above text is simply a copy of the intro of the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Building_%28mathematics%29"} wikipedia entry
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/toddtrimble/published/Buildings+for+category+theorists"} Buildings for category theorists
          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            This page is intended to be a supplementary exposition of the page
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/toddtrimble/published/Buildings+for+category+theorists"} Buildings for category theorists
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coxeter+group"} Coxeter group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            be a Coxeter group and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            the set of generators, which may be finite or infinite: In case we need
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to be finite we will mention it explicitly. We will call the tuple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (W, S)
            a
            %strong Coxeter system
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi l
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l_S
            be the
            %strong length function
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            with respect to
