          %h3#buildings_from_three_viewpoints Buildings from three Viewpoints
          %p The three viewpoints are distinguished by how one thinks about chambers.
          %h4#simplicial_complexes Simplicial Complexes
          %p In Tits’ original approach, a building is a simplicial complex satisfying additional axioms. Chambers are maximal simplices.
          %h4#combinatorial_approach Combinatorial Approach
          %p Note that this is not a firmly established term in the literature. This more modern approach originated with
          %ul
            %li [Jaques Tits: A local approach to buildings, The geometric vein (C. Davis, B.Grnbaum, and F. A. Sherk, eds.), Springer-Verlag, New York, 1981, pp. 519547.]
          %p Buildings are viewed as sets of chambers with a Coxeter-group-valued distance function satisfying certain axioms. Chambers are elements of an abstract set - more is not needed in the definition. As an alternative, chambers can be viewed as vertices of a graph. This is the point of view we will expand in future versions of this page.
          %h4#buildings_as_metric_spaces Buildings as Metric Spaces
          %p The following paper
          %ul
            %li [M. W. Davis, Buildings are CAT(0), Geometry and cohomology in group theory (P. H. Kropholler, G. A. Niblo, and R. Sthr, eds.), London Mathematical Society Lecture Note Series, vol. 252, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1998, pp. 108123.]
          %p explains that every building has a geometric realization that admits a CAT(0) metric, which means that it has metric properties analogous to those of complete simply connected Riemannian manifolds of nonpositive curvature.
          %ul
            %li [Wikipedia: CAT(k) spaces]
          %p From this viewpoint chambers are metric spaces.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#according_to_the_combinatorial_approach According to the Combinatorial Approach
          %p This definition is due to
          %ul
            %li [Jaques Tits: Twin buildings and groups of Kac-Moody type, Groups, combinatorics &amp; geometry (M. Liebeck and J. Saxl, eds.), London Mathematical Society Lecture Note Series, vol. 165, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1992, pp. 249286.]
          %p Let (W, S) be a Coxeter system (see above).
          %p
            %strong Definition:
          %p
            A
            %strong building of type (W, S)
            is a pair (C,
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "," do
              %strong chambers
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            W subject to the following axioms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                WD1, “identity of indiscernibles”:
                = succeed "(C," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %li
              %p
                WD2, “triangle inequality”: Let A, B, C be chambers and
                = succeed "(A," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                = succeed "(C," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                = succeed "(C," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                = succeed "(sw)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi l
                          %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l_S
                = succeed "(w)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi l
                          %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l_S
                = succeed "(C," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %li
              %p
                WD3, “existence of geodesics”: Let A, B be chambers and
                = succeed "(A," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                S there is a chamber C such that
                = succeed "(C," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                = succeed "(C," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
          %p This definition is equivalent from the one usually given from the simplicial viewpoint, but we will not prove that here, see e.g. the book by Abramenko and Brown in the introductory references. In the following paragraphs we will explain a few simple consequences of this definition, and introduce concepts that will allow us to identify the chambers and their “distance” relation with vertices and edges of a graph respectively (TODO: not done yet).
          %p
            But first let us note that the axioms WD1 and WD2 are somewhat similar to the axioms defining the distance function of a metric space, one notable difference is that the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            of a building takes values in the Coxeter group W of the building rather than in the nonnegative real numbers. For this reason, from the combinatorial viewpoint, buildings are sometimes called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong W-metric spaces
          %p
            In order to distinguish the two viewpoints, which is, given their equivalence, strictly speaking not necessary, some authors will talk about
            %strong W-metric buildings
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %strong simplicial buildings
          %p
            %strong Remark:
            If the Coxeter group W is finite, the building is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong spherical
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n := |S|
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℜ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+sphere"} unit sphere
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℜ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re^n
